1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,750
The official’s breath caught as his son fell silent.

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:09,787
My son… he is dying,” Nasi whispered, his voice trembling like a reed in a storm.

3
00:00:09,787 --> 00:00:17,075
He thrust his hands forward, eyes wide with terror, and demanded, “Lord, come to us before he dies!

4
00:00:17,075 --> 00:00:21,875
What would the Messiah do when a father’s hope hung on a single, desperate plea?

5
00:00:21,875 --> 00:00:29,450
The crowd had been moving through the dusty streets of Capernaum, murmuring about the Teacher who healed the leper and made the paralytic walk.

6
00:00:29,450 --> 00:00:38,875
Nasi, a dignified man in a fine tunic, had heard the rumors in the market—how a carpenter’s son was teaching with authority that made the scholars uneasy.

7
00:00:38,875 --> 00:00:45,712
He had come with a retinue of servants, his robes rustling against the stone, his mind a storm of anxiety.

8
00:00:45,712 --> 00:00:53,350
The palace of his house stood on a hill, its walls echoing with the soft cries of a child whose fever had risen like a furnace.

9
00:00:53,350 --> 00:01:00,862
The night before, the boy’s skin had grown hot, his breath shallow, his eyes glazed with the pallor of impending death.

10
00:01:00,862 --> 00:01:10,562
Nasi had sent messengers to physicians, but their herbs had failed, and now his pride, his status, could not shield him from the grief that knotted his stomach.

11
00:01:10,562 --> 00:01:18,600
When they reached the place where Jesus stood, the Messiah was surrounded by a few curious listeners, his gentle eyes scanning the crowd.

12
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:23,612
The sun had just begun its descent, casting a golden hue over the sand.

13
00:01:23,612 --> 00:01:32,375
Nasi pushed through, his sandals kicking up small clouds of dust, and fell to his knees before the man whose presence seemed to quiet even the wind.

14
00:01:32,375 --> 00:01:36,675
Lord,” he cried, “my son lies at the point of death.

15
00:01:36,675 --> 00:01:40,362
Come and lay your hand upon him, that he may live.

16
00:01:40,362 --> 00:01:46,450
Jesus looked up, his face serene, the warm olive skin catching the last rays of light.

17
00:01:46,450 --> 00:01:52,000
He did not rise to speak at length; his voice was soft yet carried the weight of authority.

18
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,650
Go; your son will live.

19
00:01:54,650 --> 00:01:59,187
The words fell like a balm, yet the air remained thick with tension.

20
00:01:59,187 --> 00:02:03,987
Nasi’s heart hammered against his ribs, a drumbeat of hope and fear.

21
00:02:03,987 --> 00:02:08,687
How could a simple command change the fate of a child already slipping away?

22
00:02:08,687 --> 00:02:16,912
The messenger who had been sent ahead to fetch the boy paused at the doorway, his hand trembling as he held the boy’s small, fevered hand.

23
00:02:16,912 --> 00:02:23,225
The house fell into a hushed silence, every breath held as if the very walls were waiting for a sign.

24
00:02:23,225 --> 00:02:29,237
At that very hour, as the sun slipped behind the hills, the boy’s eyes fluttered open.

25
00:02:29,237 --> 00:02:39,287
The fever broke, his cheeks flushed with the color of fresh figs, and a quiet gasp escaped his lips—an exhalation of life that seemed to echo through the courtyard.

26
00:02:39,287 --> 00:02:46,475
Nasi fell to his knees again, this time with tears streaming down his face, not of sorrow but of astonishment.

27
00:02:46,475 --> 00:02:51,462
Truly, you are the Son of God,” he whispered, his voice breaking.

28
00:02:51,462 --> 00:03:00,587
His servants gathered around, their faces lit with the same awe, and the household, which had been shrouded in dread, now burst into quiet rejoicing.

29
00:03:00,587 --> 00:03:06,175
The old women who had been praying at the doorway clutched each other, whispering prayers of thanks.

30
00:03:06,175 --> 00:03:12,125
Yet, even as joy swelled within the walls, a subtle unease lingered in Nasi’s mind.

31
00:03:12,125 --> 00:03:19,825
He had seen the miracle, he had felt the power of the words, but the world outside his home was still watching, still waiting.

32
00:03:19,825 --> 00:03:25,000
What would the people of Capernaum think of a nobleman who had been healed by a carpenter’s son?

33
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,750
Would they accept this new authority, or would they turn their suspicion toward him?

34
00:03:29,750 --> 00:03:35,537
The night deepened, and the household lit lamps that flickered like tiny stars against the stone.

35
00:03:35,537 --> 00:03:43,487
Nasi’s wife, still trembling, placed a hand on the child’s forehead, feeling the warmth of life where there had been only cold.

36
00:03:43,487 --> 00:03:48,625
We have witnessed a sign,” she said, “but the road ahead is still unknown.

37
00:03:48,625 --> 00:03:57,112
The question hung in the air, unspoken yet palpable: how far would this new belief carry them, and what would the Messiah demand of them next?

38
00:03:57,268 --> 00:04:04,218
In the distance, a low murmur rose from the streets—people gathering, whispers spreading like wind through wheat.

39
00:04:04,168 --> 00:04:11,330
Some spoke of the healed boy, others of the man who had spoken with authority that shook the very foundations of tradition.

40
00:04:11,330 --> 00:04:18,968
Nasi, still kneeling, lifted his eyes toward the horizon, where the silhouette of a hill loomed against the darkening sky.

41
00:04:18,968 --> 00:04:30,355
A sense of destiny brushed his cheek, and he wondered: would his son’s recovery be the beginning of a larger journey, one that would lead him beyond the safety of his own house into the very heart of the Messiah’s mission?

42
00:04:30,355 --> 00:04:38,355
The answer would not be found in the quiet of his home, but in the path that lay ahead, where the next encounter would test his newfound faith.

43
00:04:38,355 --> 00:04:47,193
The sun had not yet climbed high when Jesus slipped out of the modest home in Capernaum, his sandals brushing the dust‑laden road that led toward Nazareth.

44
00:04:47,193 --> 00:04:55,418
The narrow lane wound between white‑washed walls, and a few early risers paused, eyes widening as the carpenter’s son passed.

45
00:04:55,418 --> 00:05:03,930
He entered the town’s synagogue, a modest stone building whose wooden doors stood ajar, the faint scent of incense still clinging to the air.

46
00:05:03,930 --> 00:05:13,305
Inside, the benches were already occupied by men in tunics and women wrapped in shawls, their faces a mix of curiosity and quiet expectation.

47
00:05:13,305 --> 00:05:20,705
Jesus took the scroll of the prophet Isaiah from the priest’s hand, his fingers steady as he unrolled the parchment upon the lectern.

48
00:05:20,705 --> 00:05:24,943
With a voice that seemed to echo from the very walls, he read:

49
00:05:24,943 --> 00:05:39,405
The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because He has anointed me to bring good news to the poor; He has sent me to proclaim liberty to the captives, and recovery of sight to the blind, to set at liberty those who are oppressed, to proclaim the acceptable year of the Lord.

50
00:05:39,405 --> 00:05:44,180
A murmur rippled through the crowd, a low tide of questioning glances.

51
00:05:44,180 --> 00:05:50,118
Some whispered, “What does this mean for us?” while others shifted uneasily in their seats.

52
00:05:50,118 --> 00:05:55,993
The tension coiled like a spring, and a sharp question hung in the air, waiting to be answered.

53
00:05:55,993 --> 00:06:07,280
Jesus lifted his eyes, his dark gaze sweeping over the assembled faces, and with a calm that seemed to draw the very breath from the room he said, “Today this Scripture has been fulfilled in your hearing.

54
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:13,818
The words fell like a stone into a still pond, sending concentric waves of surprise through the listeners.

55
00:06:13,818 --> 00:06:22,993
A few nodded, recalling the miracles they had heard whispered about beyond the hills, but many furrowed their brows, their voices rising in disbelief.

56
00:06:22,993 --> 00:06:32,868
Who is this man that claims to be the fulfillment of the prophet?” demanded a voice from the back, a man whose beard was flecked with gray, his tone edged with accusation.

57
00:06:32,868 --> 00:06:38,043
This is the carpenter’s son,” another replied, a hint of scorn in his tone.

58
00:06:38,043 --> 00:06:44,918
He speaks as if he were the Messiah, yet he grew up among us, knows the market stalls and the gossip of the town.

59
00:06:44,918 --> 00:06:53,543
The crowd’s agitation grew; the older men clutched their staffs tighter, the women covered their mouths, and a restless energy began to stir.

60
00:06:53,543 --> 00:07:02,805
A young man, his eyes burning with a mixture of fear and anger, stepped forward, his hand outstretched as if to seize the very words that had unsettled him.

61
00:07:02,805 --> 00:07:09,205
If you are truly anointed,” he shouted, “prove it, or be cast out like a stone from the road!

62
00:07:09,205 --> 00:07:14,880
The question hung like a blade, poised to cut the fragile calm that had held the room together.

63
00:07:14,880 --> 00:07:18,055
In an instant the murmuring turned to a roar.

64
00:07:18,055 --> 00:07:23,893
Several men rose from their benches, their faces hardening, and surged toward the lectern.

65
00:07:23,893 --> 00:07:36,118
Hands reached for Jesus, their grip rough against his simple robe, and they dragged him toward the doorway, where the stone wall gave way to a narrow path that led down a steep slope ending at the cliffs beyond the town.

66
00:07:36,118 --> 00:07:39,193
The crowd’s chant grew louder, “Down!

67
00:07:39,193 --> 00:07:44,355
Down!” as they pressed him forward, their feet thudding against the packed earth.

68
00:07:44,355 --> 00:07:51,930
The cliff loomed, a sheer drop that disappeared into the shadowed valley below, the wind whispering warning across its edge.

69
00:07:52,084 --> 00:07:55,534
Yet as they pushed, Jesus did not stumble.

70
00:07:55,484 --> 00:08:04,184
He turned his head, his eyes meeting those of a trembling boy who had slipped into the synagogue unnoticed, his small hand clutched to his chest.

71
00:08:04,184 --> 00:08:09,234
With a voice that rose above the clamor, Jesus said, “You have heard Me.

72
00:08:09,234 --> 00:08:12,821
Let those who are without sin cast the first stone.

73
00:08:12,821 --> 00:08:16,546
The words struck the crowd like a sudden flash of light.

74
00:08:16,546 --> 00:08:22,159
Some faltered, the momentum of their assault breaking as doubt flickered across their faces.

75
00:08:22,159 --> 00:08:30,021
In that breath‑held moment, Jesus stepped aside, his feet finding the narrow path with a surety that seemed to part the very stones.

76
00:08:30,021 --> 00:08:39,609
He walked through the crowd, his mantle brushing the shoulders of those who had tried to push him, and emerged onto the ledge where the wind howled louder, the abyss yawning below.

77
00:08:39,609 --> 00:08:46,784
The townspeople’s shouts turned to gasps as they saw him stand at the brink, his silhouette framed against the darkening sky.

78
00:08:46,784 --> 00:08:52,834
A few fell back, stumbling over one another, their courage evaporating like mist in the sun.

79
00:08:52,834 --> 00:08:59,471
The man who had first seized him, now trembling, whispered, “What will become of us if He is truly sent?

80
00:08:59,471 --> 00:09:03,834
The question lingered, an unanswered prayer hanging in the thin air.

81
00:09:03,834 --> 00:09:15,159
As the crowd hesitated, a sudden, unexpected movement caught their eyes—a lone goat, its coat white as the clouds, stepped onto the path, its hooves clacking against the stone.

82
00:09:15,159 --> 00:09:19,259
The animal seemed to pause, as if waiting for a sign.

83
00:09:19,259 --> 00:09:29,059
Jesus turned his gaze to the goat, then back to the people, and with a gentle smile said, “The kingdom of God is not of this world, but its truth walks among you.

84
00:09:29,059 --> 00:09:35,271
He stepped away from the edge, his sandals finding firm ground on the path that led back into the town.

85
00:09:35,271 --> 00:09:41,971
The crowd, still trembling, watched as he passed, their anger dissolving into a bewildered silence.

86
00:09:41,971 --> 00:09:52,521
A murmur rose among them, a question that would not be answered here: would they follow this man beyond the cliffs, or would the fear of the unknown keep them bound to the narrow streets of Nazareth?

87
00:09:52,521 --> 00:10:01,034
As he disappeared through the doorway, the echo of his footsteps faded, leaving the townspeople standing at the threshold of their own convictions.

88
00:10:01,034 --> 00:10:08,746
The cliff, still looming, seemed to watch over them, a reminder that the path ahead was fraught with danger and wonder alike.

89
00:10:08,746 --> 00:10:16,234
And in the distance, beyond the stone walls of Nazareth, a road wound toward Capernaum, where another miracle awaited.

90
00:10:16,234 --> 00:10:25,984
The question now pressed upon every heart: would the people of Nazareth let the Messiah’s words settle in their souls, or would the memory of the cliff drive them to seek him elsewhere?

91
00:10:25,984 --> 00:10:36,384
The sun had already begun to bleed orange across the roofs of Capernaum when Jesus and his small band slipped through the narrow gate, the dust of the road still clinging to their sandals.

92
00:10:36,384 --> 00:10:44,834
The house of Simon—known to all as Peter—stood at the edge of the market, its shutters drawn, a low cough drifting from within.

93
00:10:44,834 --> 00:10:49,884
Peter hurried to the doorway, his eyes wide with a mixture of hope and fear.

94
00:10:49,884 --> 00:10:56,909
Master,” he called, his voice trembling, “my mother‑in‑law lies sick; the fever has taken her breath.

95
00:10:56,909 --> 00:11:03,971
Jesus stepped forward, his mantle brushing the worn wooden frame, and placed a hand gently on the woman’s forehead.

96
00:11:03,971 --> 00:11:10,084
Take her away; she is healed,” he said, the words soft yet carrying the weight of authority.

97
00:11:10,084 --> 00:11:18,209
The fever broke as if a storm had passed, and the woman sat up, her eyes clearing, a faint smile spreading across her lips.

98
00:11:18,209 --> 00:11:25,109
Peter fell to his knees, tears streaming, whispering, “Lord, you have mercy beyond our understanding.

99
00:11:25,109 --> 00:11:33,034
The house filled with quiet gratitude, but as the shadows lengthened, a murmur rose from the streets that would test the healer’s compassion.

100
00:11:33,196 --> 00:11:46,358
Word of the miracle spread faster than the evening breeze, and before the sun slipped completely beneath the horizon, a throng of villagers gathered at the entrance of the home, clutching children, crutches, and bundles of herbs.

101
00:11:46,308 --> 00:11:53,771
The door, once a simple wooden barrier, now stood as a gateway to hope, its hinges groaning under the weight of the desperate.

102
00:11:53,771 --> 00:12:00,271
Lord, we have brought all who are ill,” someone shouted, the crowd’s voice a low tide of pleading.

103
00:12:00,271 --> 00:12:07,958
Jesus moved among them, his hand resting lightly on foreheads, on shoulders, on the backs of those writhing in pain.

104
00:12:07,958 --> 00:12:14,446
A man with a burning fever felt the heat melt away as Jesus breathed on him, “Your fever is gone.

105
00:12:14,446 --> 00:12:19,921
A child with a limp stood, the pain in his legs fading as the healer’s touch lingered.

106
00:12:19,921 --> 00:12:26,796
Laughter, soft at first, rose from the crowd like a hymn, then swelled into a chorus of astonishment.

107
00:12:26,796 --> 00:12:37,246
Yet among them, a figure shivered at the edge of the doorway, his gaunt form cloaked in tattered rags, his eyes fixed on the miracle before him, his hope hanging on a single word.

108
00:12:37,246 --> 00:12:44,421
The man was Sārī, a leper whose skin was a map of sores, each one a testament to a life lived on the margins.

109
00:12:44,421 --> 00:12:51,296
He had watched from the shadows, his breath shallow, his heart beating a frantic rhythm that matched the drum of the crowd.

110
00:12:51,296 --> 00:13:00,796
When the healer turned his gaze toward the fringe of the assembly, Sārī stepped forward, his knees scraping the stone, and fell to his face before the Son of God.

111
00:13:00,796 --> 00:13:05,183
If you are willing,” he whispered hoarsely, “you can make me clean.

112
00:13:05,183 --> 00:13:11,783
The words trembled, half‑lost in the evening air, yet they landed squarely on Jesus’ compassionate heart.

113
00:13:11,783 --> 00:13:16,658
I am willing,” Jesus replied, his voice steady, “be clean.

114
00:13:16,658 --> 00:13:20,983
He lifted his hand, and the very air seemed to pulse around them.

115
00:13:20,983 --> 00:13:31,421
In an instant, the sores vanished, the raw flesh smoothed, and Sārī’s eyes widened as he felt the weight of his disease lift, replaced by a lightness he had never known.

116
00:13:31,421 --> 00:13:38,021
The leper’s voice, now clear, rose in a gasp of gratitude, “Lord, you have restored me!

117
00:13:38,021 --> 00:13:46,233
The crowd fell silent, the night air thick with awe, as if the heavens themselves were holding their breath, waiting for the next revelation.

118
00:13:46,233 --> 00:13:54,621
The night settled over Capernaum, the stars blinking awake as the last of the sick rose, healed, and embraced those who had prayed for them.

119
00:13:54,621 --> 00:14:03,658
Peter, still kneeling beside his mother‑in‑law, lifted his eyes to the man who had turned sorrow into song, his heart pounding with a newfound reverence.

120
00:14:03,658 --> 00:14:08,321
Teach us, Lord,” he whispered, “to have the faith you have shown.

121
00:14:08,321 --> 00:14:14,971
Jesus smiled, the corners of his dark eyes softening, and placed a gentle hand on Peter’s shoulder.

122
00:14:14,971 --> 00:14:20,633
Your faith has already begun,” he said, “let it grow as the light grows in the darkness.

123
00:14:20,633 --> 00:14:31,158
Around them, the townspeople gathered, voices mingling in a chorus of thanks, the leper now standing among them, his skin radiant, his story a living testament to mercy.

124
00:14:31,158 --> 00:14:40,983
The house, once dim and confined, glowed with candlelight and the warmth of healed bodies, a sanctuary of hope that would be remembered long after the sun rose again.

125
00:14:40,983 --> 00:14:50,396
The day’s miracles had drawn a line through the hearts of Capernaum, sealing a promise that the Son of God walked among them, bearing compassion for all who would seek it.

126
00:14:50,396 --> 00:14:54,171
Next time… the leper and the man through the roof.

127
00:14:54,171 --> 00:14:56,021
God bless you.

